# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for TaskSmack tests
#
# GoogleTest (v1.15) uses pthread_barrier internally for test-thread
# synchronization.  SDL3 also spawns background threads during video
# initialization.  The interaction between those threads and gtest's barrier
# code triggers false-positive data-race reports from TSAN on the
# pthread_barrier_destroy / pthread_barrier_wait entry points.
# These races are in third-party code, not in TaskSmack itself.
#
# NOTE: these are function-name suppressions, not stack-scoped ones - they hide any race
# TSan reports through these two functions anywhere on the stack, not just the specific
# gtest/SDL3 interaction described above. TaskSmack code does not itself call
# pthread_barrier_*, so this is not currently masking a real bug, but re-check this file
# if a future dependency change routes a genuine race through the same function names.
race:pthread_barrier_destroy
race:pthread_barrier_wait

# Mesa's llvmpipe (libgallium), the software GL driver CI's Xvfb display tests run on, is not
# instrumented: its fence teardown on the main thread (inside a glTexImage2D the title bar's icon
# upload makes) and its own worker threads' pthread_cond_broadcast on the same fence read to TSan as
# a race (#2042). TSan matches "race:" against each frame's function, file and module name, so this
# hides only reports with a libgallium frame on the stack; TaskSmack has no code in that module.
race:libgallium

# libsystemd is not instrumented either. When two threads read the journal at once (the crash
# history's concurrent-read test, #2416), its first-use setup of a module-global random seed writes
# through the getrandom interceptor on both threads, which TSan reports as a race on a libsystemd
# global. sd_journal handles are per-probe; the shared state is libsystemd's own. This hides only
# reports with a libsystemd frame on the stack; TaskSmack has no code in that module.
race:libsystemd
